# =============================================================================
# Section 27's C/H with a LOCALLY NESTED ROW spliced into its outer layer.
# 162 blocks, 28704 cells, 0 inverted.
#
# This is the videos' method put into a mesh worth having, rather than into the
# purpose-made demonstrator of section 11 (24 stations, straight radials, no
# clustering, max 89.6 / mean 30.5). make_ch.py is NOT touched -- the nest is
# spliced into its OUTPUT by nest_into.py -- so the eight cases beside this one
# cannot regress, and the wall clustering, the curvature and the far field are
# all section 27's.
#
# THE NEST IS IN THE OUTER LAYER, not against the wall, and that is structural
# rather than a preference: each termination station's corner on the ring ABOVE
# must be omitted (section 11a), and in the outermost layer that ring is the FAR
# FIELD, where the two circumferential edges either side merge into one and
# nothing propagates further. Put it in the wall layer and the omission has to
# be merged through every layer outside it.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, against the same case without the nest:
#   inverted      0          ->  0
#   non-ortho max 50.062 deg ->  89.87      <-- ONE near-degenerate corner
#   non-ortho mean 1.840 deg ->  3.25
#   wall first cell          ->  unchanged, 0.0276
# The max is at the left transition block, at (0.036, -0.971), and it does NOT
# move: measured at --band 0.15/0.30/0.45 it is 89.98/89.92/89.87, so it is the
# transition itself and not where the nested ring sits. Grading the two end
# stations back toward the far field was tried as the fix and is WORSE --
# --taper 0.3/0.5/0.7 gives 7/13/26 inverted cells.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py \
#       --layers 2 --split 32 --turn-blend 0.8 --wake 8 --out base.json
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/nest_into.py base.json \
#       --at 20 --band 0.30 --out gridpro_ch_nested.json
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_nested.dat
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case.
#
MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_nested.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- the rectangle is declared, corner by corner.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/naca0012_cgrid.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing: the far-field and outlet edges
# carry no binding, so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar. A real case would want
# inlet / outlet / farfield told apart, which needs a far-field geometry file.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family (section 8g); five is
# where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_ch_nested/mesh_gridpro_ch_nested.vtk
